      "textSnapshot": "Problem:\nentire trail update could only reach a trail that already had a branch — either by passing --branch or by defaulting to the current branch. A branchless trail has no branch to match, so nothing could reach it, and nothing could give it one.\nSolution:\n--trail selects a trail by number, id, or branch. A branchless trail matches no branch, so the number or id form is what reaches it.\n--set-branch attaches a branch to a branchless trail through the /branch endpoint, either linking an already-pushed branch (--branch-action link, the default) or creating it on the forge at the trail's base (--branch-action create). New API types TrailBranchRequest and TrailBranchResponse model that endpoint.\nExample:\n❯ entire trail update --trail 3 --set-branch henlo\nAttached branch henlo to trail #3\n\n❯ entire trail update --trail 3 --set-branch compatible-change\ntrail #3 is already on branch henlo and a trail's branch cannot be changed once set; create a trail for compatible-change instead\nTwo server rules the CLI now enforces and explains:\nA trail's branch cannot be changed once set. --set-branch on a trail that already has a branch is refused before anything is sent; asking for the branch it already has does nothing, so re-running a command stays safe. The CLI deliberately does not send branch on the metadata PATCH: that endpoint accepts a raw branch write and checks neither rule, so using it would route around both.\nOne branch backs one trail. The /branch endpoint answers 409 for a branch another trail already holds, and the CLI says what the rule is instead of passing the bare server text through.\nValidation happens before any server-side change. Deciding what --set-branch means is split from carrying it out: planTrailBranchChange takes no API client and so cannot reach the forge, and the attach runs after every other input has been checked. An invalid --title or --status alongside --branch-action create no longer leaves a created branch behind.\nWhen an attach lands but a later PATCH fails, the error says the branch was attached and points at the retry that works, rather than reading as though nothing happened.\n--trail and --branch cannot be combined. --trail wins outright when resolving, so accepting both would quietly update a trail other than the one --branch named. This matches show, comment and approval.\nTests cover the branch decision and each input it rejects, both 409 messages, the attach request and response decoding, the partial-state wrapper, and the flag clash.\nmain is merged in. Every conflict was in this command, where main had grown assignee, reviewer, type and priority flags alongside these branch flags. Main also replaced the single inline PATCH with splitTrailUpdate and sendTrailPatch, so the nothing-left-to-send check now asks splitTrailUpdate instead of a hand-listed set of changed fields that had gone stale against main's new flags.\nProblem:\nentire trail update could only reach a trail that already had a branch — either by passing --branch or by defaulting to the current branch. A branchless trail has no branch to match, so nothing could reach it, and nothing could give it one.\nSolution:\n--trail selects a trail by number, id, or branch. A branchless trail matches no branch, so the number or id form is what reaches it.\n--set-branch attaches a branch to a branchless trail through the /branch endpoint, either linking an already-pushed branch (--branch-action link, the default) or creating it on the forge at the trail's base (--branch-action create). New API types TrailBranchRequest and TrailBranchResponse model that endpoint.\nExample:\n❯ entire trail update --trail 3 --set-branch henlo\nAttached branch henlo to trail #3\n\n❯ entire trail update --trail 3 --set-branch compatible-change\ntrail #3 is already on branch henlo and a trail's branch cannot be changed once set; create a trail for compatible-change instead\nTwo server rules the CLI now enforces and explains:\nA trail's branch cannot be changed once set. --set-branch on a trail that already has a branch is refused before anything is sent; asking for the branch it already has does nothing, so re-running a command stays safe. The CLI deliberately does not send branch on the metadata PATCH: that endpoint accepts a raw branch write and checks neither rule, so using it would route around both.\nOne branch backs one trail. The /branch endpoint answers 409 for a branch another trail already holds, and the CLI says what the rule is instead of passing the bare server text through.\nValidation happens before any server-side change. Deciding what --set-branch means is split from carrying it out: planTrailBranchChange takes no API client and so cannot reach the forge, and the attach runs after every other input has been checked. An invalid --title or --status alongside --branch-action create no longer leaves a created branch behind.\nWhen an attach lands but a later PATCH fails, the error says the branch was attached and points at the retry that works, rather than reading as though nothing happened.\n--trail and --branch cannot be combined. --trail wins outright when resolving, so accepting both would quietly update a trail other than the one --branch named. This matches show, comment and approval.\nTests cover the branch decision and each input it rejects, both 409 messages, the attach request and response decoding, the partial-state wrapper, and the flag clash.\nmain is merged in. Every conflict was in this command, where main had grown assignee, reviewer, type and priority flags alongside these branch flags. Main also replaced the single inline PATCH with splitTrailUpdate and sendTrailPatch, so the nothing-left-to-send check now asks splitTrailUpdate instead of a hand-listed set of changed fields that had gone stale against main's new flags.",
